# Partial Compliance Equals Non-Compliance

Skills 1, 3, and 4 each instantiate the same high-altitude rule in a different engineering phase: partial adherence to a requirement is treated as a failure, not a partial success. In implementation (skill 1), updating one coupled layer while leaving others unchanged violates the invariant just as completely as updating none. In pre-work preparation (skill 3), reading some plans or some paths is not better-than-nothing — the missed paths are exactly where bugs hide. In testing (skill 4), asserting one observable proxy when the full content must be verified gives the same false confidence as no assertion. The session-spanning principle: wherever a completeness requirement exists, there is no partial credit — you are either complete or you are wrong.

## Procedures
- For each requirement or invariant, define its completion boundary explicitly before starting.
- Treat any gap within that boundary (unread plan, unchecked layer, untested observable) as a full violation, not a minor omission.
- Surface gaps loudly rather than accepting partial work as 'good enough for now'.

